WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.153
This is Cairo.

00:00:02.153 --> 00:00:04.725
Egypt’s largest city and its capital.

00:00:05.893 --> 00:00:09.591
For decades it's been home to Egyptian rulers
and their palaces.

00:00:09.591 --> 00:00:13.054
The Parliament has met in this building
for over 150 years.

00:00:13.054 --> 00:00:17.823
And this public square in the heart of Cairo
has been the site of several revolutions.

00:00:18.991 --> 00:00:22.600
But in 2015, the Egyptian government
announced the capital would move

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.580
about 50 kilometers away...

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:26.492
To this patch of empty desert.

00:00:26.492 --> 00:00:30.852
Which is quickly shaping up to be the
New Administrative Capital of Egypt.

00:00:30.852 --> 00:00:33.163
This is where a new presidential palace 
will go...

00:00:33.163 --> 00:00:36.244
the new Parliament building and
a new public square.

00:00:36.244 --> 00:00:39.590
Egypt’s government, led by president
Abdel Fattah El-Sisi

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:42.580
claims this new city will solve 
a specific problem:

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.200
Cairo’s overpopulation.

00:00:47.723 --> 00:00:52.556
But the country has a long history of building
new cities to decongest Cairo.

00:00:52.556 --> 00:00:54.673
Many of them sit outside the city today.

00:00:56.527 --> 00:01:00.718
So, why is Cairo’s population still considered
a ticking time bomb?

00:01:02.553 --> 00:01:05.709
And what’s the real reason behind 
this new capital?

00:01:21.562 --> 00:01:25.315
There's a population counter
outside one of the ministries.

00:01:25.315 --> 00:01:27.150
It's mundane, but it also

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:31.460
it's red, flashing lights with these colors
is almost always like telling people...

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.667
A little too many of you are here.

00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:35.399
This is Mohamed Elshahed

00:01:35.399 --> 00:01:38.367
an architectural historian who studies
urban development in Egypt.

00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:44.812
And I find this to be quite a dangerous narrative
especially since that's actually not the case.

00:01:44.812 --> 00:01:47.319
Egypt's home to more than 100 million people.

00:01:47.319 --> 00:01:50.754
And about 20% of them live within the boundaries
of Greater Cairo.

00:01:50.754 --> 00:01:52.819
The city has one of the highest 
population densities

00:01:52.819 --> 00:01:53.819
in the world

00:01:53.819 --> 00:01:57.659
with 153,000 people per square kilometer
at its peak.

00:01:57.659 --> 00:02:00.666
That’s higher than New York, London, and
Shanghai.

00:02:00.666 --> 00:02:04.509
What we know as Cairo, today, was officially
founded on the banks of the Nile

00:02:04.509 --> 00:02:07.840
in the 10th century, as the capital of
the Arab Fatimid Caliphate.

00:02:08.359 --> 00:02:10.259
The city had to be built near the river

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:12.877
because the rest of the region 
was barren desert.

00:02:12.877 --> 00:02:16.010
Over the next several centuries, each ruler
built expansions of Cairo

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:17.682
close to the fertile banks.

00:02:17.682 --> 00:02:19.335
The Ottomans built these areas...

00:02:19.335 --> 00:02:23.189
and the British added these suburbs during
their decades-long occupation of Egypt.

00:02:23.189 --> 00:02:26.720
In the 1950s, when the British left and
Egypt became a republic

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.960
Cairo was by far the biggest city.

00:02:31.074 --> 00:02:35.329
In the 50s, 60s and 70s, millions of impoverished
Egyptians from the countryside

00:02:35.329 --> 00:02:39.394
moved to Cairo in search of opportunities
and better living conditions.

00:02:39.394 --> 00:02:42.040
But the city that was built haphazardly along
the river

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:43.868
wasn't equipped to house more people.

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:47.120
The city sort of was already kind of
shaped and demarcated

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.560
and the green areas around it were left agricultural.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.120
And it's those areas that were cheap

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.328
and close enough to those amenities that allowed
then people to step in.

00:02:58.328 --> 00:03:03.065
Since the housing that was available 
was completely not sufficient.

00:03:03.065 --> 00:03:07.120
So farmers started selling small slices of
their agricultural land to the migrants.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.900
A lot of people have to build for themselves
with little money.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.030
So together, so entire families would go vertical
on a small plot of land.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:17.730
These were the first informal and
technically illegal neighborhoods.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:22.459
It's informal because it's not, you know,
given licenses for construction.

00:03:22.459 --> 00:03:27.129
It's built outside of the economy, that is
the “official economy".

00:03:27.129 --> 00:03:30.153
And I think this is actually the result of
the fact that

00:03:30.153 --> 00:03:34.796
the economic system really excluded 
the majority of the population.

00:03:34.796 --> 00:03:37.923
For decades, Egypt's government failed
to build affordable housing

00:03:37.923 --> 00:03:41.661
or invest in public services and infrastructure
to support the explosive growth

00:03:41.661 --> 00:03:43.305
in Cairo’s density.

00:03:43.305 --> 00:03:46.049
And that meant these unplanned and
informal neighborhoods

00:03:46.049 --> 00:03:49.770
continued to get more and more crowded
until it became a crisis.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.239
"Egypt’s capital is bursting at the seams."

00:03:52.239 --> 00:03:55.200
"Cairo is equipped to handle around 3 million,
in fact,

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.609
it's home to 8 million."

00:03:56.609 --> 00:03:59.049
"Housing is an area of desperate need."

00:03:59.049 --> 00:04:01.950
"Many people in Cairo live in makeshift shacks

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:05.543
in the city's center and in much worse conditions
in the suburbs."

00:04:06.118 --> 00:04:10.272
Today, these informal neighborhoods are home
to 60% of Cairo’s population.

00:04:11.739 --> 00:04:13.700
Most of them are inhabited by the poor

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:16.500
but many have evolved into middle class neighborhoods.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.863
Collectively, these are the most crowded areas
of Cairo.

00:04:19.863 --> 00:04:22.560
And Egypt’s government, under Sisi, points
to them

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.000
as the real problem behind Cairo's overcrowding.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.630
I find this to be a very problematic narrative
because then we take a question of

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:34.885
let's say, bad design or bad management from
a state perspective

00:04:34.885 --> 00:04:38.673
and turn the blame on actually the people
who are suffering

00:04:38.673 --> 00:04:41.150
from the bad design and the bad policies

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:42.920
and say, there's just too many of you.

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:47.127
In fact, the government still considers them
illegal, referring to them as slums.

00:04:47.127 --> 00:04:52.520
From the point of view of the state, calling
an area a slum facilitates its removal.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:57.486
In 2019, the government announced it would
eradicate Cairo's slums by 2030.

00:04:57.486 --> 00:05:00.000
Many neighborhoods will be redeveloped
as affluent housing

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.120
while thousands of residents will be forcibly
pushed out of their homes

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.104
and into affordable housing outside the city.

00:05:06.104 --> 00:05:08.894
But instead of focusing on sufficient
affordable housing

00:05:08.894 --> 00:05:12.298
they invested billions of dollars in
a whole new city elsewhere.

00:05:13.578 --> 00:05:16.001
And it's not the first time an Egyptian ruler

00:05:16.001 --> 00:05:19.269
has tried to solve the crisis by starting
fresh in the desert.

00:05:20.498 --> 00:05:22.487
"A building boom is in progress."

00:05:22.487 --> 00:05:25.380
"New apartment blocks are going up 
at a greater rate

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.483
since the start of Nasser’s Revolution."

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:32.970
The concept of a new city as a solution for
Cairo's growing population began in the 1950s.

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:35.870
The first attempt was under President
Gamal Abdel Nasser

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:39.750
who ordered the construction of a new city
called Nasr City.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:42.170
It was also designed to be a new capital 
at the time.

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:45.960
There would be space for government buildings,
markets, and a huge new stadium.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:47.594
But there was a big flaw.

00:05:47.594 --> 00:05:49.560
The plan didn't involve affordable housing

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:52.175
for the people in Cairo's most crowded areas.

00:05:52.843 --> 00:05:56.371
The need was for a lot of lower class housing.

00:05:56.371 --> 00:05:59.539
But that wasn't going to be placed in the
new capital.

00:05:59.539 --> 00:06:03.952
Nobody wants to build a shiny new capital
and then fill it with low cost housing.

00:06:03.952 --> 00:06:06.702
So it's sort of a trend that already 
starts there.

00:06:06.702 --> 00:06:10.000
By the 1970s, Egypt had a new leader, 
Anwar Sadat

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.662
who didn’t end up moving the capitol to
Nasr City.

00:06:12.662 --> 00:06:16.431
But he really leaned into the trend of building
‘desert cities’.

00:06:16.431 --> 00:06:19.980
Starting in 1976, Egypt’s government built
8 new cities

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.060
in the desert around Cairo.

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:25.000
Each time they claimed the goal was
to alleviate overcrowding.

00:06:25.799 --> 00:06:28.375
But, most included only expensive housing.

00:06:28.375 --> 00:06:31.035
And barely any featured public transportation

00:06:31.035 --> 00:06:34.262
making them inaccessible for anyone 
without a car.

00:06:34.262 --> 00:06:38.021
That’s why today, many of these desert cities
are only partially occupied.

00:06:38.021 --> 00:06:41.305
Most who have moved here are Cairo’s
middle and upper class residents.

00:06:41.305 --> 00:06:45.100
That means that the majority of the population
that's squeezed in the little green belt

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:49.497
that's now urbanized around the Nile are kind
of X’d-out of these developments.

00:06:49.497 --> 00:06:52.220
So we're looking at a condition 
that's a result of

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:54.730
I would say, 3 to 4 decades 
in particular of

00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:56.280
misguided policy that

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:58.510
looks to the outside of the city.

00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:01.762
President Sisi is continuing that trend
with his new capital.

00:07:01.762 --> 00:07:04.170
These areas are designated for housing, but
mostly

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:06.420
for middle and upper class residents.

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:08.780
Those people are not moving in with handouts.

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:11.590
They're buying their those properties.

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:15.485
And so the main target audience is, again,
a moneyed class.

00:07:15.485 --> 00:07:20.110
I think the housing for low-income communities
will be included in the capital

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:21.932
but definitely very limited.

00:07:21.932 --> 00:07:24.110
All of this land will be used for 
government buildings

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.050
and this section will be a business district

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:28.424
featuring this soon-to-be-tallest 
building in Africa.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.850
So, if this new capital isn’t really about
solving Cairo’s population density crisis

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:37.110
why is the government so determined 
to build it?

00:07:44.201 --> 00:07:48.300
In 2011, protests erupted in Egypt
over police brutality.

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:50.150
And they quickly evolved into widespread calls

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:52.894
for the resignation of Egypt’s ruler 
of 30 years,

00:07:52.894 --> 00:07:54.620
Hosni Mubarak.

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:56.700
Demonstrations took place all over Egypt.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:58.760
But the biggest were in Cairo.

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:00.470
Specifically, Tahrir Square.

00:08:01.212 --> 00:08:03.620
It’s been the epicenter of many political
demonstrations

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:05.956
since the early 20th century.

00:08:05.956 --> 00:08:10.508
Largely because it’s so close to Egyptian
government buildings, including the Parliament.

00:08:10.508 --> 00:08:14.452
In 2011, millions of people occupied 
Tahrir Square for 18 days.

00:08:14.452 --> 00:08:15.700
They set up roadblocks...

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:17.667
and battled with police.

00:08:17.667 --> 00:08:20.480
On February 11, thousands marched from 
Tahrir Square

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:23.332
to the presidential palace, 10 km away

00:08:23.332 --> 00:08:27.100
where they ultimately forced Mubarak 
to step down.

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:29.850
The ability to control Tahrir Square 
and inner Cairo

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:33.039
allowed the protestors to effectively paralyze
the government.

00:08:33.039 --> 00:08:37.117
Making their presence and their demands
impossible to ignore.

00:08:37.117 --> 00:08:39.620
Sisi remembers that.

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:43.456
He was the head of Egypt’s powerful military
when he seized power in 2013

00:08:43.456 --> 00:08:45.374
on the heels of the revolution.

00:08:45.968 --> 00:08:47.710
Barely a month after taking power

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:49.760
he commanded his soldiers and police 
to crack down

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:51.380
on protestors who opposed him.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:54.234
The brutal raid killed more than 800 people.

00:08:54.234 --> 00:08:56.760
Ever since, he's been systematically trying
to prevent

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:58.660
a revolution that could topple him.

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:03.110
His government has shut down political dissents,
punished journalists, and hindered free speech.

00:09:03.815 --> 00:09:06.200
Now, they are redesigning several aspects
of Cairo

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:08.610
to make the city harder to protest in.

00:09:08.962 --> 00:09:11.140
They’ve widened dozens of streets

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:12.700
making it more difficult to erect road-blocks.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:15.550
And they plan to add 40 bridges

00:09:15.550 --> 00:09:19.370
which will give the military and police
easier access to the city center.

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:22.530
Sisi’s government has also renovated 
Tahrir Square

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:25.000
adding giant monuments and 
private security guards

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:28.971
which, some experts, say will make it harder
for large crowds to gather.

00:09:28.971 --> 00:09:30.815
Now, they’re taking the final step:

00:09:30.815 --> 00:09:33.480
removing the government entirely from Cairo.

00:09:34.574 --> 00:09:37.560
Just 7 years after announcing the construction
of the new capital

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:41.270
government officials are starting to move
into these new buildings.

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:45.030
Once that’s complete, all of Egypt’s 
political power, including Sisi

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:48.220
will be concentrated here... almost 
50 kilometers from Cairo.

00:09:48.925 --> 00:09:50.874
And it will all be under heavy guard:

00:09:50.874 --> 00:09:53.397
this is the military's sprawling complex nearby.

00:09:53.842 --> 00:09:56.690
Even though there is a public square,
called the People’s Piazza

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:58.340
it’s hard to imagine millions of people

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:01.304
making the journey from Cairo 
to demonstrate here.

00:10:01.304 --> 00:10:03.349
And that may be the point.

00:10:03.349 --> 00:10:05.900
The distance, plus the military presence

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:10.120
will allow Sisi to defend his power
in case of an uprising.

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:13.813
So instead of a capital that offers a solution
for the people of Cairo

00:10:13.813 --> 00:10:17.154
this city is designed to move the government
out of their reach.

